WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
Use-after-free defects often appear in error handling and cleanup code.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:14.120
The ordinary success path may be straightforward, while failure paths release only some resources, jump across state updates, or continue after cleanup.

3
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.300
Defensive review should give error paths the same attention as the main path.

4
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:24.100
Partial initialization requires cleanup logic that knows which resources are currently valid.

5
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:32.520
A function might allocate one object, initialize a second object, register a callback, then fail while preparing a third step.

6
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:37.420
Cleanup should release only what was acquired and should leave the remaining state predictable.

7
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.620
Releasing resources in multiple places can create accidental reuse or repeated cleanup.

8
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:47.800
A pointer might be freed in a helper, then freed again by the caller.

9
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:53.980
A function might clean up on error and then fall through to code that assumes the object is still active.

10
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.560
These problems are not about one line of code; they are about unclear control flow.

11
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.920
A predictable single cleanup path can reduce lifetime confusion.

12
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:09.500
Many C codebases use a structured cleanup section that releases resources in reverse acquisition order.

13
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:16.320
This approach is not magic, but it gives reviewers one place to inspect ownership state and makes early exits easier to reason about.

14
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.520
Cleanup code should leave objects in a safe, well-defined state.

15
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:29.660
That may mean clearing an owner pointer, resetting count and capacity metadata, unregistering callbacks before release, or marking a structure as no longer initialized.

16
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.520
The right state depends on whether the object can be reused, returned, or only discarded.

17
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.760
After cleanup, later code should not continue as though the object is still usable.

18
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:49.820
Watch for log calls, callbacks, metrics updates, error formatting, or cleanup helpers that use fields from an object after its storage has been released.

19
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.760
If information is needed after cleanup, copy the safe summary before release.
